<tr><th>Code</th><th>Valid?</th><th>What the compiler does</th></tr>
<tr><td>boolean b = true;</td><td>valid</td><td>prints true</td></tr>
<tr><td>boolean b = 0;</td><td>invalid</td><td>incompatible types — found: int, required: boolean. (0 is an integral value → default int. In C, 0 means false; not in Java.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>boolean b = True;</td><td>invalid</td><td>`cannot find symbol`. True is not a boolean value, not an int, not a String (no double quotes). Compiler treats it as a variable named True that was never declared. Symbol: variable `True`, location: class `Test`.</td></tr>
<tr><td>boolean b = &quot;true&quot;;</td><td>invalid</td><td>incompatible types — found: java.lang.String, required: boolean. You cannot provide a String where a boolean primitive is expected.</td></tr>
</table>
